<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud Gawan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1411">1411</a>–<a href="/years/1481">1481</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Khwaja Mahmud Gawan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud Gawan was a Persian merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> who became chief minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, serving from the 1460s until 1481. He reorganised the provinces and revenue system, led campaigns that extended Bahmani territory to the west coast, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capture-of-goa">capture of Goa</a>, and founded a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>. Accused of treason through a forged letter, he was executed on the orders of Muhammad Shah III in 1481.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-gawan.jpg" alt="Mahmud Gawan" width="320" height="183" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Prasannasindol · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Complete_view_of_Mahumad_Gawan.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Imadu'd-din Mahmud in 1411 in the village of Gawan, he came, according to the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firishta">Firishta</a>, from a family of ministers of a kingdom in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a>; by his own account it had included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">viziers</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a>. After intrigues undermined the family, he and his brother Shihabu'd-din Ahmad left Gilan on their mother's advice. For more than a decade he travelled as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, trading in horses and other goods and studying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. In 1453 he landed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dabhol">Dabhol</a> to sell horses to the Bahmanis, and Sultan Ahmad Shah II received him at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> and made him a noble.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Humayun Shah made him chief minister, Wakil-us-Sultanat, with the title Prince of Merchants. After Humayun's death he sat with the queen mother and the noble Jahan Turk on the regency council of the boy sultans Nizam Shah and Muhammad III, which helped repel a Gajapati invasion and, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-begada">Mahmud Begada</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>, the attacks of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-khalji">Mahmud Khalji</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a>. When the queen mother had Jahan Turk killed in 1466, she gave Mahmud supervision of all the provinces and the title of prime minister.</p><p>From 1469 he campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, taking the Konkan and in February 1472 the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goa">Goa</a>, partly to curb piracy against the horse trade and Muslim pilgrims. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belagavi">Belgaum</a> he made the first known use of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>. In 1473 he divided the provinces from four into eight, shared between the local Deccani and foreign Afaqi nobles, and limited each governor to a single fort, reforms that many nobles resented. He wrote poetry and letters, 148 of which survive, corresponding with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, the Timurid Husayn Bayqara and the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jami">Jami</a>. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a> held a library of 3,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Rival nobles forged letters, apparently in Mahmud's hand, inviting the Gajapati king Purushottamadeva to invade. Muhammad Shah III, partly drunk, rejected Mahmud's plea that they were forged and had him executed on 5 April 1481. The sultan later regretted it and died a year afterwards; Malik Hasan Bahri, one of the plot's chief architects, succeeded Mahmud as prime minister. The disorder that followed led to the independence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bijapur">Bijapur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar">Ahmadnagar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berar">Berar</a> sultanates in 1490 and of the Bidar Sultanate in 1492.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mahmud Gawan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the minister <b>Mahmud Gawan</b> the army conquered the Konkan coast and took Goa from Vijayanagara in 1472.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iran">Iran</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidar">Bidar</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/india">India</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.28,49.59&amp;year=1456" aria-label="Gilan Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5791 822 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Mahmud Gawan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6241 1092h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6241 1092h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.28,49.59&amp;year=1456">What was here in 1456? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
